;; Docs for std/test.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "test.bjodoc"). The texts
;; are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Tests that are silent unless they fail: deftest, check, and run-tests as the file's main.")
  (see deftest check run-tests)
  (example """
    (import (std prelude))
    (import (std test))

    (deftest "parse adds"
      (check (= (parse "1+2") (Ok 3))))

    (deftest "a bad number is an error"
      (check-match (parse "x") (Err _)))

    (run-tests)
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{deftest} registers a test where it is written. @code{(run-tests)}
    is the @code{main} of the file: it runs every test that the module
    registered, in source order. A test that passes prints nothing. A test
    that fails prints its name, its file and line, and each check in it that
    failed, with the values that the check saw. The run ends with one line,
    and the program exits with 1 if a test failed:

    @codeblock[#:lang "text"]|{
    FAIL parse adds (parse_test.bjo:4)
      parse_test.bjo:5: (check (= (parse "1+2") (Ok 3)))
        (parse "1+2") => (Ok 4)
        (Ok 3)        => (Ok 3)
    1 of 2 test(s) failed in 12 ms.
    }|

    @strong{Choosing tests.} The arguments of the program choose the tests.
    A test runs when its name contains one of the arguments. With no
    arguments, every test runs. Arguments that start with @code{--} are
    flags, such as @code{--seed} and @code{--quick}: see
    @code{parse-test-args}. @code{bjo test} builds and runs the test files
    that it is given, or every file under @code{tests/} in the project. It
    gives its other arguments to each file.

    @strong{Options.} A test can be skipped (@code{#:skip}), expected to
    fail (@code{#:todo}), left out of quick runs (@code{#:slow}), or run
    once for each row of a table (@code{#:cases}). See @code{deftest}.

    @strong{More kinds of tests.} @code{(std test prop)} has property tests:
    @code{for-all} runs a body with many generated values, and shrinks a
    failing case to a small one. @code{(std test gen)} has the generators.
    @code{(std test expect)} compares output with a text in the test, and
    @code{--promote} updates the text.

    @strong{Each test runs in a scope of its own}, as @code{with-test} in
    @code{(std simpletest)} does. The scope has a deadline: 5 seconds, or
    @code{#:within d}, a Duration. The scope waits for the fibers that the test
    started. The test fails if it leaves a resource open, unless it has
    @code{#:open-ok}. A test that raises an exception fails with the message
    of the exception, and the tests after it still run. A failed check does
    not stop its test, so one run shows all the failed checks.

    @strong{Each module runs only its own tests.} Every module that imports
    this one registers into the same list. A test records the module it was
    written in, from @code{(current-module)}, and @code{(run-tests)} runs
    only the tests of its module. Thus a test file can import a library that
    has tests of its own, and it does not run them.

    @strong{(std test) and (std simpletest).} @code{(std simpletest)} is the
    older harness. Its @code{expect} prints a line for each assertion, and a
    failure is a line that starts with @code{FAILURE:}, which is what the
    compiler's own test runner looks for. It has no registry: a test file is
    an ordinary program that calls the assertions from @code{main}. Use
    @code{(std test)} for new tests and with @code{bjo test}. The two can be
    used together: @code{with-fake-fs} from @code{(std simpletest)} works
    inside a @code{deftest}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Tests and their registry
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Test
  (summary "A registered test: its name, where it is, its options and its body.")
  (field name "The name given to @code{deftest}. The filters of a run match against it.")
  (field module "The key of the module that registered it, as @code{(current-module)} gives it.")
  (field file "The path of the source file that it is written in.")
  (field line "The line of the @code{deftest}.")
  (field within "The deadline in milliseconds. 0 means the default, 5000.")
  (field open-ok "#t when the test can leave resources open.")
  (field skip "The reason given with @code{#:skip}, or empty. A test with a reason does not run.")
  (field todo "The reason given with @code{#:todo}, or empty. A failure of such a test does not fail the run.")
  (field slow "#t for a @code{#:slow} test, which @code{--quick} leaves out.")
  (field capture "#t when the body uses @code{expect-output}. The output of the test then goes to a string port that @code{take-test-output!} reads.")
  (field body "The forms of the test, as a function. @code{run-test} calls it.")
  (see deftest run-test registered-tests))

(:doc test-name
  (summary "The name of a test.")
  (arg t "The test.")
  (returns "The name given to @code{deftest}."))

(:doc test-module
  (summary "The module that registered a test.")
  (arg t "The test.")
  (returns "The key of the module, as @code{(current-module)} gives it.")
  (see module-tests))

(:doc test-file
  (summary "The source file of a test.")
  (arg t "The test.")
  (returns "The path of the file, as the compiler read it.")
  (see test-line))

(:doc test-line
  (summary "The line of a test's deftest.")
  (arg t "The test.")
  (returns "The line number.")
  (see test-file))

(:doc register-test!
  (summary "Adds a test to the registry. deftest writes calls to it.")
  (arg name "The name of the test.")
  (arg module "The key of the module, from @code{(current-module)}.")
  (arg file "The source file.")
  (arg line "The line of the test.")
  (arg within "The deadline in milliseconds, or 0 for the default.")
  (arg open-ok "#t when the test can leave resources open.")
  (arg skip "The reason of @code{#:skip}, or empty.")
  (arg todo "The reason of @code{#:todo}, or empty.")
  (arg slow "#t for @code{#:slow}.")
  (arg capture "#t when the body uses @code{expect-output}.")
  (arg body "The body of the test.")
  (returns "Always 0.")
  (see deftest)
  (reference """
    It answers a number so that @code{deftest} can bind the result with a
    top-level @code{def}. A top-level @code{def} runs before @code{main},
    so all the tests are registered before @code{(run-tests)} runs them.
    It is exported because the template of @code{deftest} names it. Call
    @code{deftest}, not this function.
    """))

(:doc registered-tests
  (summary "Every test registered so far, by every module, in the order they were registered.")
  (returns "The tests, oldest first.")
  (see module-tests))

(:doc module-tests
  (summary "The tests that one module registered, in the order they were written.")
  (arg module "The key of the module, as @code{(current-module)} gives it in that module.")
  (returns "The tests of that module, oldest first.")
  (see registered-tests run-registered-tests)
  (example """
    (module-tests (current-module))   ; the tests of this module
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The options of a run
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc TestOptions
  (summary "What the arguments of a test program asked for.")
  (field filters "Parts of names. A test runs when its name contains one of them. When empty, every test runs.")
  (field seed "The seed of the run: from @code{--seed}, from the environment variable BJO_TEST_SEED, or from the operating system.")
  (field runs "The number of runs of a @code{for-all} that does not give @code{#:runs}, from @code{--runs}. 0 when not given.")
  (field shrink-limit "The most replays that shrinking does, from @code{--shrink-limit}. 0 when not given.")
  (field stats "#t with @code{--stats}: print the counts of @code{classify} and @code{collect}.")
  (field quick "#t with @code{--quick}: leave out the @code{#:slow} tests.")
  (field promote "#t with @code{--promote}: write the output that was seen into the texts of @code{expect-output}.")
  (field store "#f with @code{--no-store}: do not read or write the failure file of @code{(std test prop)}.")
  (field junit "The file of @code{--junit}, to write the results to as JUnit XML.")
  (see parse-test-args current-test-options))

(:doc parse-test-args
  (summary "Reads the arguments of a test program: flags, and name filters.")
  (arg args "The arguments.")
  (returns "The options, or why the arguments are wrong.")
  (see TestOptions run-registered-tests)
  (reference """
    An argument that starts with @code{--} is a flag. The other arguments
    are name filters.

    @itemlist{
      @item{@code{--seed n}: the seed of the run, in decimal or as
        @code{0x} hex. A failed property prints the seed, so that the run
        can be repeated.}
      @item{@code{--runs n}: the number of runs of a @code{for-all} that
        gives no @code{#:runs}.}
      @item{@code{--shrink-limit n}: the most replays that shrinking does.}
      @item{@code{--junit file}: also write the results to the file as
        JUnit XML.}
      @item{@code{--stats}: print the counts of @code{classify} and
        @code{collect}.}
      @item{@code{--quick}: leave out @code{#:slow} tests.}
      @item{@code{--promote}: write the output that was seen into the texts
        of @code{expect-output}.}
      @item{@code{--no-store}: do not read or write the failure file.}
    }

    Without @code{--seed}, the seed comes from the environment variable
    BJO_TEST_SEED, and else from the operating system. @code{bjo test}
    gives these flags to each test file: @code{bjo test --seed 7}.
    """))

(:doc current-test-options
  (summary "The options of the run in progress.")
  (returns "The options that @code{run-registered-tests} read, or the options of a run without arguments when no run is in progress.")
  (see TestOptions))

(:doc test-seed
  (summary "The seed of one test: the seed of the run, mixed with the test's name.")
  (arg t "The test.")
  (returns "The seed.")
  (see mix-seed string-hash)
  (reference """
    Because the name is part of the seed, choosing tests with filters, or
    adding a test, does not change what the other tests draw.
    """))

(:doc mix-seed
  (summary "Two seeds mixed into one, so that seeds that differ in one bit give unrelated results.")
  (arg a "A seed.")
  (arg b "Another seed.")
  (returns "The finalizer of SplitMix64, applied to a xor b."))

(:doc string-hash
  (summary "A 64-bit hash of a string that is the same in every process.")
  (arg s "The string.")
  (returns "FNV-1a over the characters of s.")
  (reference """
    .NET's own string hash is different in each process, so it can not make
    a seed that has to be the same in every run.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Failures
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc running-test
  (summary "The test that is running.")
  (returns "The test, or None when no test runs.")
  (see run-test))

(:doc test-time-left
  (summary "The milliseconds left before the running test's deadline.")
  (returns "The time left, or a day when no test runs.")
  (see running-test))

(:doc take-test-output!
  (summary "What the running test wrote since it started, or since the last call.")
  (returns "The text, or None when the test does not capture its output.")
  (see test-time-left)
  (reference """
    A test captures its output when its body uses @code{expect-output}:
    @code{current-output-port} is then a string port while the test runs.
    What no call took is printed when the test ends, so that the prints of
    a test are not lost.
    """))

(:doc next-test-index!
  (summary "The number of times this was called before with the same key in the running test.")
  (arg key "What is counted: @code{for-all} gives the source text of its bindings.")
  (returns "0 at the first call with a key in a test, then 1, 2 and so on.")
  (reference """
    @code{for-all} takes its position among the @code{for-all}s of its test
    that have the same bindings from it, to find the cases that the failure
    file holds for it.
    """))

(:doc failures-into
  (summary "Runs a function with the failures of its checks going to a box instead of to the running test.")
  (arg failures "The box. Each failure is added at its front.")
  (arg body "The function.")
  (returns "What the function answers.")
  (see test-failed!)
  (reference """
    The failures of the running test are put back afterwards, also when
    the function raises an exception. @code{for-all} runs each case this
    way, to decide which failures to keep.
    """))

(:doc test-failed!
  (summary "Records a failure of the running test, or prints it and fails the program when no test runs.")
  (arg message "The text of the failure, printed as it is given.")
  (see check-failed! run-test)
  (reference """
    Use it to write a check of your own. In a test, the message is added to
    the failures of the test, and the test goes on. Outside a test, the
    message is printed at once, and the exit code of the program is set to
    1, so that the program fails even when @code{main} answers 0.

    The run prints a failure under the @code{FAIL} line of its test, so
    start the message with two spaces to align it with the other failures.
    """))

(:doc check-failed!
  (summary "Records the failure of a check: where it is, its source, and the values it saw.")
  (arg file "The source file of the check.")
  (arg line "The line of the check.")
  (arg source "The source text of the check.")
  (arg seen "Pairs of source text and value, one pair for each value to show.")
  (see test-failed! check)
  (reference """
    The checks of this module call it. It is exported because their
    templates name it. It formats the failure as below, with the source
    texts padded to the same width, and gives it to @code{test-failed!}.
    Only the file name of @code{file} is shown.

    @codeblock[#:lang "text"]|{
      file.bjo:12: (check (= a b))
        a => 1
        b => 2
    }|
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Checks
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc check
  (summary "Passes when the expression is #t. A failed comparison shows both operands.")
  (form (check expr))
  (arg expr "The expression to test.")
  (see check-match check-raises check-fail)
  (example """
    (check (= (parse "12") (Ok 12)))
    (check (< (length xs) 10))
    (check (string-starts-with? s "a"))
    """)
  (reference """
    When @code{expr} is a call to @code{=}, @code{<}, @code{>}, @code{<=}
    or @code{>=} with two operands, each operand is evaluated once, in
    order, and a failure shows the source and the value of both. For any
    other expression, a failure shows that the expression was @code{#f}.

    A failed check does not stop the test. Outside a test, a failure is
    printed at once and the program exits with 1.
    """))

(:doc check-match
  (summary "Passes when the value of the expression matches the pattern. A failure shows the value.")
  (form (check-match expr pattern))
  (arg expr "The expression. It is evaluated once.")
  (arg pattern "A @code{match} pattern. The names that it binds are not available after the check.")
  (see check)
  (example """
    (check-match (parse "x") (Err _))
    (check-match (lookup m "k") (Some (Tuple _ 3)))
    """))

(:doc check-raises
  (summary "Passes when the expression raises an exception of the type.")
  (form (check-raises type expr))
  (form (check-raises type expr #:message part))
  (arg type "A .NET exception type, by its name or by an @code{import/class} alias.")
  (arg expr "The expression to evaluate.")
  (arg part "A string that the message of the exception has to contain.")
  (see check)
  (example """
    (check-raises System.FormatException (string->int "x"))
    (check-raises System.ArgumentException (make-inbox 0 DropNewest) #:message "capacity")
    """)
  (reference """
    When @code{expr} answers a value, the check fails and shows the value.
    Only exceptions of @code{type} are caught. An exception of a different
    type is not caught, so the test fails as a test that raises does, and
    the checks after it in the same test do not run.
    """))

(:doc check-fail
  (summary "Fails where it is: for a branch that a test must not reach.")
  (form (check-fail why))
  (arg why "A string that tells why the branch is wrong. The failure shows it.")
  (see check)
  (example """
    (match (parse "12")
      ((Ok n) (check (= n 12)))
      ((Err e) (check-fail e)))
    """))

(:doc check-raises-message
  (summary "The message part of check-raises: fails when the message does not contain the part.")
  (arg file "The source file of the check.")
  (arg line "The line of the check.")
  (arg source "The source text of the check.")
  (arg message "The message of the exception.")
  (arg part "What it has to contain.")
  (see check-raises)
  (reference """
    It is exported because the template of @code{check-raises} names it.
    Write @code{check-raises} with @code{#:message}, not this function.
    """))

(:doc approx-close?
  (summary "Whether two doubles are near each other, as check-approx tells.")
  (arg a "A double.")
  (arg b "Another double.")
  (arg eps "The bound.")
  (returns "#t when they are equal, or differ by at most eps times the larger of 1 and their sizes.")
  (see check-approx)
  (reference """
    It is exported because the template of @code{check-approx} names it.
    """))

(:doc check-approx
  (summary "Passes when two doubles are near each other. A failure shows both.")
  (form (check-approx a b))
  (form (check-approx a b #:eps eps))
  (arg a "A double. It is evaluated once.")
  (arg b "A double. It is evaluated once.")
  (arg eps "The bound. 1e-9 when it is not given.")
  (see check)
  (example """
    (check-approx (sqrt 2.0) 1.4142135623730951)
    (check-approx (mean xs) 2.5 #:eps 1e-6)
    """)
  (reference """
    Two values are near when they are equal, or when they differ by at most
    @code{eps}, for values at most 1 in size, or by at most @code{eps}
    times the larger of the two sizes, for larger values. So the bound is
    absolute near 0 and relative away from it. Equal infinities are near.
    NaN is near nothing, also NaN.
    """))

(:doc check-output
  (summary "Passes when what the expression writes to current-output-port is the text.")
  (form (check-output expr text))
  (arg expr "The expression. Its value is dropped.")
  (arg text "A string: what it has to write.")
  (see check)
  (example """
    (check-output (display "hi") "hi")
    (check-output (print-report r) "total: 3\n")
    """)
  (reference """
    The expression writes to a string port instead of the real output, so
    nothing is printed. A failure shows what it wrote and what was wanted.
    For longer output, @code{expect-output} from @code{(std test expect)}
    shows a diff, and @code{--promote} updates the text.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; deftest and run-tests
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc deftest
  (summary "Registers a test. (run-tests) runs it.")
  (form (deftest name body ...))
  (form (deftest name #:within d body ...))
  (form (deftest name #:open-ok body ...))
  (form (deftest name #:skip why body ...))
  (form (deftest name #:todo why body ...))
  (form (deftest name #:slow body ...))
  (form (deftest name #:cases ((column ...) row ...) body ...))
  (arg name "A string: the name of the test. The filters of a run match against it.")
  (arg d "The deadline of the test, a Duration. Five seconds when it is not given.")
  (arg why "A string: the reason, which the run shows.")
  (rest column "The names that each row binds.")
  (rest row "A list of values, one for each column.")
  (rest body "The forms of the test. At least one form. Their values are dropped.")
  (see run-tests check run-test)
  (example """
    (deftest "parse adds"
      (check (= (parse "1+2") (Ok 3))))

    (deftest "the server answers" #:within (seconds 1)
      (check-match (fetch url) (Ok _)))

    (deftest "parse-int" #:cases ((input expected)
                                  ("1"  (Ok 1))
                                  ("-0" (Ok 0))
                                  ("x"  (Err _)))
      (check-match (parse-int input) expected))

    (deftest "unicode case folding" #:todo "needs full case folding"
      (check (string-ci=? "STRASSE" "straße")))
    """)
  (reference """
    Write it at the top level of a module: it expands to a top-level
    @code{def}, so the test is registered before @code{main} runs. The
    options come after the name, in any order.

    The test runs in a scope with a deadline, as @code{run-test} tells.

    @itemlist{
      @item{@code{#:within d} sets the deadline, a Duration: @code{#:within (seconds 10)}.}
      @item{@code{#:open-ok} turns off the check for open resources. Use it
        in a test of what the closing of a scope does.}
      @item{@code{#:skip why}: the test does not run. The last line of the
        run counts it.}
      @item{@code{#:todo why}: the test runs, and a failure does not fail
        the run. When it passes, the run prints a @code{FIXED} line, so
        that the mark can be removed.}
      @item{@code{#:slow}: @code{--quick} leaves the test out.}
      @item{@code{#:cases}: a table. The first list names the columns, and
        each list after it is a row of values. The body runs once for each
        row, with the columns bound to the row's values. A failure is
        reported with the row's number and values, and the other rows still
        run. A column that the body uses as the pattern of a
        @code{check-match} is put into the pattern instead, so a row can
        hold a pattern such as @code{(Err _)}. The rows end at the first
        form that is not a list as long as the columns, or at the last
        form, which is always the body.}
    }
    """))

(:doc TestStatus
  (summary "How a test came out.")
  (case Passed "It ran, and nothing failed.")
  (case Failed "Something in it failed.")
  (case Skipped "It has @code{#:skip}, so it did not run.")
  (case Todo "It has @code{#:todo}, and it failed, as expected. The run does not fail.")
  (case Fixed "It has @code{#:todo}, and it passed. The run says so, so that the mark can be removed.")
  (see TestResult run-test))

(:doc TestResult
  (summary "What running one test came to.")
  (field test "The test that ran.")
  (field status "How it came out.")
  (field failures "The messages of its failures, in the order they occurred. Empty when it passed.")
  (see run-test))

(:doc test-result-test
  (summary "The test that a result is for.")
  (arg r "The result.")
  (returns "The test."))

(:doc test-result-status
  (summary "How a test run came out.")
  (arg r "The result.")
  (returns "The status."))

(:doc test-result-failures
  (summary "The failures of a test run.")
  (arg r "The result.")
  (returns "The failure messages, in the order they occurred. Empty when the test passed."))

(:doc run-test
  (summary "Runs one test in a scope of its own, and answers what it came to. Prints nothing.")
  (arg t "The test.")
  (returns "Its result, with each failure as the run prints it.")
  (see run-registered-tests TestResult)
  (reference """
    The body runs under @code{with-deadline}, with the deadline of the test.
    The failures of the checks in the body are collected, not printed. The
    test also fails:

    - when the body leaves a resource open at its end, unless the test has
      @code{#:open-ok}. The check is done before the scope closes, because
      the scope releases the resources when it closes.
    - when the deadline ends the scope. A fiber that the test leaked is
      cancelled by the deadline, and the scope then closes cleanly, so
      without this the test would pass, slowly.
    - when the body raises an exception. The failure shows the message.

    Use it to run tests and examine the results, for example to test a
    check that must fail.
    """))

(:doc run-registered-tests
  (summary "Runs the tests of a module that the filters choose, prints the failures, and answers an exit code.")
  (arg module "The key of the module, as @code{(current-module)} gives it.")
  (arg args "The arguments of the program: flags, and parts of names. See @code{parse-test-args}.")
  (returns "0 when no test failed, 1 when one failed, 2 when the arguments are wrong. 0 also when no test was chosen.")
  (see run-tests run-test)
  (reference """
    For each test that fails, it prints a line @code{FAIL name (file:line)}
    and then the failures. For a @code{#:todo} test that passes, it prints
    a line @code{FIXED name (file:line)}. At the end it runs the hooks given
    to @code{at-run-end!}, writes the JUnit file when @code{--junit} asks
    for one, and prints one line: how many tests passed or failed, how many
    were skipped or todo, and the time the run took, or that there were no
    tests to run. @code{(run-tests)} writes a @code{main} that calls it.
    """))

(:doc at-run-end!
  (summary "Adds a function to what runs when a run of tests ends.")
  (arg key "A name for the hook. A second hook with the same key is ignored.")
  (arg hook "The function.")
  (see run-registered-tests)
  (reference """
    @code{run-registered-tests} runs the hooks after the last test, oldest
    first, before it prints its last line. @code{(std test prop)} writes its
    failure file this way, and @code{(std test expect)} its promotions.
    """))

(:doc deftest-cases-row
  (summary "The heading of a failed row of a #:cases test.")
  (arg i "The number of the row, from 1.")
  (arg seen "The column names, each with the source text of its value.")
  (returns "The heading, and a line for each column.")
  (reference """
    It is exported because the template of @code{deftest} names it.
    """))

(:doc run-tests
  (summary "Writes the main of a test file: it runs every test that this module registered.")
  (form (run-tests))
  (see deftest run-registered-tests)
  (example """
    (deftest "one" (check (= 1 1)))
    (run-tests)
    """)
  (reference """
    It defines @code{main}, which takes the arguments of the program as
    name filters and answers the exit code of
    @code{run-registered-tests}. Thus a test file is a program, and it can
    have no other @code{main}.

    All the tests of the module run, also the tests written after
    @code{(run-tests)}, because every @code{deftest} is registered before
    @code{main} starts. The tests of other modules, also of modules that
    this one imports, do not run.
    """))
